1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:06,140
You know, we usually imagine being locked into a piece of software as, uh, one massive catastrophic decision.

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:12,340
Like someone in your ministry signed the wrong multi-million dollar check on a Tuesday, and suddenly your systems are trapped forever.

3
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:13,260
Yeah, well,

4
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:15,020
it really is not like that.

5
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:18,660
The reality of vendor lock-in is actually much quieter.

6
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:20,880
It is, um, a trap

7
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:28,080
written clause by clause into contracts that nobody bothers to read until the exact day your program wants to leave.

8
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:31,940
Right. And suddenly, leaving becomes incredibly costly to change.

9
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:35,560
Welcome to this deep dive into sourcing digital building blocks.

10
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:43,820
Today, our mission is to explore how you can maintain flexibility and control over your systems by strategically choosing who builds them, and crucially,

11
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:46,540
uh, how you write the exit strategy for your minister.

12
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:52,280
To start, the reference architecture outlines three distinct paths for sourcing the blocks of your system.

13
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:59,080
First, you have the in-house path, which is reserved for what your own teams can realistically create and keep running.

14
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,140
Which makes sense for manageable components.

15
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:07,040
Exactly. But you pair that with outsourcing for the heavy lifting your team simply cannot do.

16
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:10,780
You know, things like complex cybersecurity or cloud services.

17
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:15,360
And you have to manage that outsourced work with a strict risk management framework, I assume.

18
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:16,020
You do.

19
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:18,920
And then the third path is partnerships,

20
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:24,580
which involves teaming up with technology firms or universities, or initiatives like GovStack.

21
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:30,360
Combining these three paths prevents your ministry from being entirely dependent on a single channel.

22
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:38,460
Wait, so if the whole point is avoiding the heavy lifting and minimizing risk, why not just grab an off-the-shelf pre-tested solution?

23
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:44,840
Doesn't a platform like the GovStack Sandbox bypass this headache instantly and give you a production ready system?

24
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:50,380
Well, it seems like it should, but the Sandbox is strictly a demonstration and learning tool.

25
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:54,420
Building digital public infrastructure is never plug and play.

26
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:57,360
Even when a product shows GovStack testing,

27
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:00,320
it only provides a compliance level of one or two.

28
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:03,220
Meaning what practically for a government official?

29
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:07,780
Meaning the vendor completed a self-assessment and some basic interface tests.

30
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:17,700
It verifies that their software plug fits the socket, but it guarantees absolutely nothing about how that system will perform under the stress of your specific live environment.

31
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:21,500
So a product listing is evidence and not a promise.

32
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:23,900
You still have to test everything in your own installation.

33
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:30,940
Right. Because if you do not, the systems become fragmented, siloed, duplicated, or rekeyed,

34
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:34,460
and the financial consequences are staggering.

35
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:42,080
A two thousand and eighteen World Bank finding showed that for identity systems, procurement choices can swing the overall cost by twenty-five percent

36
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:44,020
to over one hundred percent.

37
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:49,340
Wow. How does a cost swing of over one hundred percent actually happen mechanically?

38
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:53,720
Well, it happens when you lose leverage and become locked into one supplier.

39
00:02:54,380 --> 00:03:01,720
If a vendor owns the underlying code and the proprietary data formats, every time your program wants to add a new feature, that vendor can charge

40
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:07,140
massive fees. Because nobody else can touch the system, you have to pay whatever they ask.

41
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:16,620
Which is exactly why the twenty twenty-four UN Universal DPI Safeguards Framework points to vendor lock-in as a primary cause of unsustainability and long-term costs.

42
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:19,880
So how do we actually disarm the trap in the contract?

43
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:24,280
The framework requires four non-negotiable checks in every agreement.

44
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:27,080
First, you must mandate named open standards.

45
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:32,100
This ensures your data is written in a universal language and not a proprietary vendor code.

46
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:34,140
Right. So you are not tied down.

47
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:35,080
Exactly.

48
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,240
Second, you guarantee the export of all data and configuration.

49
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:44,160
If you leave, you need the legal right to take your blueprint and your information with you.

50
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:46,940
Leaving the vendor with an empty shell, essentially.

51
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:47,420
Yes.

52
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:50,940
And the third check is mandatory help with leaving.

53
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:57,200
The old vendor has to be contractually bound to hand over the keys and assist the new vendor during the transition.

54
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:58,760
That is crucial.

55
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:00,220
And the fourth check.

56
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:02,760
The fourth check acts as your safety net.

57
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:05,620
License terms that outlive the contract itself.

58
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:11,180
If a dispute happens and the contract terminates, the software does not just instantly shut off.

59
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:14,920
You get a legal grace period to keep the system running while you migrate.

60
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:16,760
Let us see this in action.

61
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:19,860
Take a fictional country, so let us call it Progresa.

62
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:22,580
How do they mix and match these paths?

63
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:30,620
In Progresia, they purchase a product for their registration service, but they configure it entirely in-house using their own analysts.

64
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:36,120
The contract explicitly guarantees that the service description is exportable at any time.

65
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,620
So they buy the tool, but they maintain total control over the setup and the exit strategy.

66
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:45,340
Yes. And for their identity services, they take a different route.

67
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:51,240
They do not buy anything. It is handled purely through a partnership agreement using the identity authority sign-in.

68
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:58,100
Every digital block has a specific deliberate sourcing choice and a customized exit clause.

69
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:04,840
So if your minister is drafting one of these contracts tomorrow, the main takeaway is to deliberately decide who builds each block,

70
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:11,220
but retain control by writing open standards and clear terms for leaving into every single agreement.

71
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,360
You just have to keep the keys to your own infrastructure.

72
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:19,360
Sourcing decides who builds the house, but the contract decides if you can ever leave it.

